  immediately:                             {aria: 'Addons', role: tab}  (race window, not yet processed)
  +80ms:                                   {aria: 'I agree to the terms', role: checkbox}  <-- RECLAIMED again
  +150ms / +300ms / +600ms:                stable on the checkbox

== Interpretation ==
Directly observes, live, the exact mechanism named in fix commit 1925636a3: a
collapse to a contextless idle focus state self-heals onto the fixture's own
checkbox within ~100ms, with ZERO Tab presses, from two different starting
states — and a legitimately-focused sibling control (Addons tab) is never
stolen from. This is the live counterpart of the pinned automated test
(widgetbook/test/n_checkbox_stories_test.dart, "reclaims focus after the
app-wide focus tree goes idle, without a Tab press").

== Known methodology caveat (not a product defect) ==
The visual mint InkWell.focusColor overlay (alpha .12) did NOT render as
detectable pixels in this headless/semantics-driven test session, on EITHER
the fresh-mount focus or the post-reclaim focus. Root cause (confirmed by
reading the Flutter SDK source, ink_well.dart:1148 + focus_manager.dart's
`handleSemanticsAction`): on web, ANY SemanticsAction.focus/tap (i.e. every
click routed through the accessibility-tree proxy, which is how this test
navigated/interacted) resets FocusManager.instance.highlightMode to `touch`,
and InkWell only paints focusColor when highlightMode == `traditional`
(keyboard). A real end user pressing a physical Tab key (no intervening
semantics-proxy click) would see the ring normally. This is a pre-existing
Flutter Web framework characteristic, orthogonal to this ticket's fix, and
affects PIXEL-based verification methodology only — not the underlying
focus-state correctness demonstrated above, and not the AC's own repro
mechanism (which is about focus RETURNING, not about the touch/keyboard
highlight heuristic). Flagged as a followup for future widgetbook visual QA
tooling, not a task_bug or regression_bug against this fix.
